As Bob, I argue against fare-free buses. Ann's premises are mostly reasonable: fares do weigh on low-income riders. The weakest point is what she leaves out, the side effects. Fares pay for part of a bus system's running costs, and when larger US systems went fare-free (Austin in 1989-90, Denver, Trenton) they ran into crowding, disruptive riders and schedule problems, and most went back to charging fares. The National Academies' Transit Cooperative Research Program reviewed these programs and concluded fare-free works mainly for small systems. The Side Effects question is an exception on a1, argued rather than asked, and Expert Opinion (0.70) can back any question, so I attack a1 with it. If the exception holds, a1 should be defeated and the claim should go OUT.

